WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.822
Tax policy.

00:00:01.772 --> 00:00:04.090
The wealth transfer mechanism.

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:09.954
The federal corporate tax rate has been cut from twenty-eight percent in 2000 to fifteen percent today.

00:00:09.954 --> 00:00:13.727
Capital gains are taxed at half the rate of employment income.

00:00:13.727 --> 00:00:18.068
The principal residence exemption makes housing gains completely tax-free.

00:00:18.068 --> 00:00:21.193
Stock options receive preferential treatment.

00:00:21.193 --> 00:00:24.988
Private corporations can retain earnings and split income.

00:00:24.988 --> 00:00:31.124
Meanwhile, wage earners pay marginal rates up to fifty-three percent when combined with provincial taxes.

00:00:31.124 --> 00:00:44.170
The tax system documents its own inequality: CRA data shows that the top one percent of earners hold a disproportionate share of capital gains income, while the bottom fifty percent earn primarily employment income taxed at the highest effective rates.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:48.079
Every mechanism documented here is federal tax policy.

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:54.647
Every mechanism shifts burden from capital to labour, from corporations to individuals, from the wealthy to the middle class.

00:00:54.647 --> 00:00:59.795
Five tax policy mechanisms that transfer wealth from wage earners to capital owners.

00:00:59.795 --> 00:01:01.443
The net effect.

00:01:01.443 --> 00:01:07.329
Every mechanism in the Canadian tax system that provides preferential treatment benefits capital over labour.

00:01:07.329 --> 00:01:10.193
Corporate rate cuts benefit shareholders.

00:01:10.193 --> 00:01:13.534
Capital gains inclusion rates benefit investors.

00:01:13.534 --> 00:01:17.249
The principal residence exemption benefits property owners.

00:01:17.249 --> 00:01:20.386
Stock option treatment benefits executives.

00:01:20.386 --> 00:01:24.318
CCPC structures benefit incorporated professionals.

00:01:24.318 --> 00:01:29.761
Each mechanism is federal tax policy — a deliberate policy choice made by the government.

00:01:29.761 --> 00:01:38.352
The cumulative effect is a tax system that imposes the highest effective rates on wage earners while providing preferential treatment for every form of capital income.

00:01:38.352 --> 00:01:47.806
This is the fiscal layer of institutional capture: the tax system that extracts revenue from the majority to fund the programs that serve the interests of the capital-owning minority.

00:01:47.806 --> 00:01:51.147
The PM came from Goldman Sachs and Brookfield.

00:01:51.147 --> 00:01:54.624
The tax system serves Goldman Sachs and Brookfield.
